## `upload()` (lines 1–6)
```cpp
bool upload(const std::string& url, const std::string& body) {
  try {
    auto resp = http_post(url, body);
    return resp.ok;
  } catch (...) { return false; }
}
```
- **`catch (...)` swallows every exception** from `http_post` (line 5) — network failures, timeouts, DNS errors, malformed URLs — and discards the exception type and message with no logging, no rethrow, no error code. The caller gets a bare `false` and no idea *why*.
- **It conflates two distinct failure modes.** A transport exception and a legitimate non-OK HTTP response (`resp.ok == false`) both collapse to `false`. The caller cannot distinguish "the request never reached the server" from "the server rejected it."

## `backup()` (lines 7–10)
```cpp
void backup() {
  std::ofstream f("backup.dat");
  f << data();  // write failures ignored
}
```
- **Open failure is unchecked** (line 8). If `backup.dat` can't be opened — permissions, disk full, bad path — the stream is in a failed state and nothing is reported.
- **Write failure is unchecked** (line 9). `f << data()` is never followed by a `f.good()`/`f.fail()` check, so a disk-full or I/O error is silently dropped.
- **It returns `void`**, so even a fully failed backup is indistinguishable from a successful one to the caller. The function's whole purpose (durably persisting data) can fail with zero signal.

